The library is designed to handle up to 10 million items efficiently, avoiding segmentation faults or excessive resource consumption. To achieve this, it uses a dynamic array to store the `item` structures, which allows for efficient insertion and deletion of items without reallocating memory. The `update_item_fields` function is also designed to minimize overhead during runtime by checking if the new fields are valid before applying them to all existing items in the collection.

The library supports substring matching feature that supports both exact and partial matches, allowing external clients to query the collection based on specified substrings. To balance between speed and memory usage, the search algorithm uses a simple linear search with a time complexity of O(n) for each query, where n is the number of items in the collection. However, to optimize cache utilization for large-scale data processing, the library can use techniques such as caching or precomputing the longest string in the collection and storing it separately from the main data structure.

The library also integrates a persistence layer that periodically writes the collection to disk to prevent data loss in case of a system crash. To optimize disk I/O operations, the library can use techniques such as batch writing or compressing the data before writing to reduce overhead. Additionally, the library addresses concurrency challenges by designing mechanisms to handle simultaneous read and write access from multiple clients, ensuring data integrity and consistency.

Overall, this library is designed to be modular and extensible, facilitating future enhancements and maintenance. It can be further optimized and improved based on the specific use case and performance requirements.']

