The `read_data` function is responsible for reading data from a file or network socket using an input method specified by the user. It checks if the input method is supported and calls the corresponding read function with the provided file path and fd. If the input method is not supported, it throws an error.

The `read_file` function reads data from a file using the `fopen`, `fread`, and `fclose` functions from the C standard library. It allocates memory for the file contents, reads the entire file into memory, and returns the read data to the caller.

The `read_socket` function connects to a specified socket address and port using the `socket`, `connect`, and `recv` functions from the C standard library. It receives the data from the server using the `recv` function and closes the socket. It then returns the received data to the caller.

The `read_env_var` function reads the value of a specified environment variable using the `getenv` function from the C standard library. It allocates memory for the environment variable value, copies the value into memory, and returns the copied data to the caller.

The `get_file_size` function gets the size of a file in bytes using the `fstat` function from the C standard library. It returns the file size to the caller.

The `recv_data` function receives data from a socket and closes the socket using the `recv`, `close` functions from the C standard library. It allocates memory for the received data, reads the entire file into memory, and returns the read data to the caller.']

