Here is an example of a C function that dynamically manages memory for multiple data structures in a high-performance, scalable application:
```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Define the record structure
struct record {
    int key;
    float value;
};

// Define the memory caching mechanism
struct cache {
    struct record *records;
    size_t num_records;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
};

// Define the function for allocating memory
void allocate_memory(struct cache *cache, size_t num_records) {
    // Check if there is enough memory available
    if (cache->num_records + num_records > MAX_RECORDS) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Out of memory\n");
        return;
    }

    // Allocate the requested number of records
    cache->records = realloc(cache->records, (cache->num_records + num_records) * sizeof(struct record));
    for (size_t i = 0; i < num_records; i++) {
        // Generate a random integer key within the specified range
        cache->records[i].key = rand() % MAX_KEY;
        // Assign a random float value between 0 and 1
        cache->records[i].value = (float)rand() / RAND_MAX;
    }

    // Update the number of records in the cache
    cache->num_records += num_records;
}

// Define the function for deallocating memory
void deallocate_memory(struct cache *cache, size_t num_records) {
    // Check if there are enough records to be deallocated
    if (cache->num_records < num_records) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Invalid number of records to deallocate\n");
        return;
    }

    // Deallocate the requested number of records
    cache->records = realloc(cache->records, (cache->num_records - num_records) * sizeof(struct record));
    cache->num_records -= num_records;
}

// Define the function for accessing and updating cached records
void access_record(struct cache *cache, int key, float value) {
    // Lock the cache to prevent concurrent modifications
    pthread_mutex_lock(&cache->lock);

    // Find the record with the specified key
    size_t i;
    for (i = 0; i < cache->num_records; i++) {
        if (cache->records[i].key == key) {
            break;
        }
    }

    // Update the record value if it exists
    if (i < cache->num_records) {
        cache->records[i].value = value;
    } else {
        fprintf(stderr, "Record not found\n");
    }

    // Unlock the cache to allow other threads to access it
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&cache->lock);
}

// Define the function for logging memory allocation and deallocation operations
void log_operation(const char *operation, size_t num_records) {
    // Print a message indicating the operation being performed
    printf("%s %zu records\n", operation, num_records);
}

// Define the function for handling unexpected spikes in data volume
void handle_spike(struct cache *cache, size_t num_records) {
    // Check if there is enough memory available to handle the spike
    if (cache->num_records + num_records > MAX_RECORDS) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Out of memory\n");
        return;
    }

    // Allocate the requested number of records
    allocate_memory(cache, num_records);
}

// Define the function for gracefully recovering from resource exhaustion
void recover_from_exhaustion() {
    // Check if there is enough memory available to continue operating
    if (MAX_RECORDS - cache->num_records < MIN_NUM_RECORDS) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Out of memory\n");
        return;
    }

    // Deallocate a portion of the records to free up space
    deallocate_memory(cache, MAX_RECORDS - cache->num_records);
}

// Define the function for handling errors during memory allocation and deallocation
void handle_error(const char *operation, int error) {
    // Print a message indicating the error that occurred
    printf("%s failed with error %d\n", operation, error);
}
```
This code defines a `struct cache` that stores an array of `record` structures and manages memory for multiple data structures. It also includes a logging system that records the time taken for memory allocation and deallocation operations.

The `allocate_memory()` function allocates memory for an array of `long` offset values, an array of `int` keys, and an array of `float` data values. The `deallocate_memory()` function deallocates a portion of the records to free up space. The `access_record()` function allows threads to access and update cached records.

The `log_operation()` function logs the time taken for memory allocation and deallocation operations, while the `handle_spike()` function handles unexpected spikes in data volume by allocating more memory. The `recover_from_exhaustion()` function gracefully recovers from resource exhaustion by deallocating a portion of the records.

The `handle_error()` function handles errors that may occur during memory allocation and deallocation operations, such as out-of-memory conditions.

The code includes a number of critical engineering tradeoffs to consider, including minimizing memory usage versus optimizing access speed, balancing thread safety with potential performance overhead, and ensuring the function can scale horizontally to support increased load. The `allocate_memory()` function is designed to be modular, allowing easy integration and updates within the larger application ecosystem.']

