2. Implement a function in C that initializes an AES-256-CBC cipher with a given password, using the OpenSSL library. The function should set the IV length to 16 bytes and the key length to 32 bytes, deriving the encryption key from the password via a SHA-256 hash function. Ensure that the cipher is properly configured and return a pointer to the cipher context. Implement a mechanism to manage cipher resource cleanup upon termination or when the cipher is no longer in use.
```c
#include <openssl/aes.h>
#include <openssl/sha.h>

// Define the function to initialize an AES-256-CBC cipher with a given password
int init_cipher(const char *password, AES_KEY *key) {
    // Generate a 32-byte encryption key from the password via SHA-256 hash function
    unsigned char key_buf[32];
    SHA256(password, strlen(password), key_buf);

    // Set the IV length to 16 bytes and the key length to 32 bytes
    AES_set_IV(key, key_buf, 16);
    AES_set_key(key, key_buf + 16, 32);

    // Check if the cipher was properly configured
    if (AES_set_encrypt_key(key) != 0) {
        return -1;
    }

    // Return a pointer to the cipher context
    return key;
}
