For thread-safe access to shared resources, you can use locks or other synchronization mechanisms to ensure that multiple threads accessing the same resource do not interfere with each other. Here is an example of how you could implement a spin lock:
```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <assert.h>

// Spin lock for synchronizing access to the shared resource
typedef struct {
    int locked;  // Indicates whether the lock is held by any thread
} spin_lock_t;

void acquire_spin_lock(spin_lock_t* lock) {
    while (1) {
        if (!lock->locked) {
            lock->locked = 1;
            return;
        }

        // Busy-wait for the lock to become available
        continue;
    }
}

void release_spin_lock(spin_lock_t* lock) {
    if (lock->locked) {
        lock->locked = 0;
    }
}
```
This spin lock implementation uses a simple busy-waiting approach to acquire the lock, where one thread waits in a loop until it is able to acquire the lock. The `acquire_spin_lock` function returns immediately if the lock is already available, or waits indefinitely until it becomes available. The `release_spin_lock` function sets the locked flag back to 0 when it releases the lock.

To handle memory exhaustion scenarios gracefully, you can use a dynamic resizing strategy that allows for the addition of new records and the rebalancing of existing ones. Here is an example implementation of such a strategy:
```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
