The `add_record()` function adds a new record to the `records` array by locking the mutex, checking if there is enough space in the array to store the new record, and then adding it. It returns the index of the new record. The `get_record()` function retrieves a record by its index and returns it if found. The `update_record()` function updates the value, offset, and weight of a record by its index, while also locking the mutex to ensure thread safety. Finally, the `delete_record()` function deletes a record from the `records` array by locking the mutex and updating the indices of the other records.

This implementation uses a predefined threshold of 10 million records to trigger a reallocation of memory when the number of records exceeds it. This allows for efficient management of memory allocation and avoids excessive memory consumption. The function also supports dynamic resizing of records if the number of records exceeds the predefined threshold during runtime, while minimizing performance impact.

