To implement a flexible and extensible logging manager in C that dynamically discovers and initializes logging mechanisms for writing logs to various destinations, including files, network servers, and in-memory buffers, we will need to consider several design principles and implementation strategies. Here is an outline of the steps involved in developing this system:

1. Define a plugin architecture for adding new logging backends without modifying the core logging manager. This will allow third-party developers to contribute new logging mechanisms without requiring changes to the core codebase.
2. Implement dynamic loading and dependency management to load logging libraries dynamically, resolving symbol dependencies and managing memory efficiently. Support loading from shared object files on Linux and DLLs on Windows. Ensure that the manager can handle version mismatches and incompatible interfaces gracefully.
3. Use a resource pool for logging mechanisms to minimize memory overhead and improve performance. Ensure that resources are allocated and deallocated efficiently, with careful consideration of thread safety and concurrent access. Use synchronization primitives to protect shared resources and ensure thread-safe operations.
4. Optimize the manager for high-throughput logging scenarios by balancing time and space complexity. Consider techniques such as batching, compression, and asynchronous processing to maximize throughput while minimizing latency. Evaluate the impact of different buffer sizes and batching intervals on performance.
5. Seamlessly integrate with an existing logging framework, allowing for efficient operation within a larger system. Define clear interfaces for communication between the logging manager and other components, ensuring that the manager can be easily extended and maintained. Address potential integration points where the logging manager might conflict with or augment existing logging capabilities.
6. Provide comprehensive logging and error handling mechanisms to facilitate debugging and monitoring. Log successful operations and capture errors for analysis, ensuring that the manager can be easily maintained and extended in the future. Support detailed logging of performance metrics to monitor the manager's efficiency and identify bottlenecks.
7. Support asynchronous logging to avoid blocking critical paths in the application. Implement mechanisms to queue log messages and process them in a non-blocking manner, ensuring that the manager remains responsive under heavy load. Evaluate the trade-offs between using a dedicated logging thread and a thread pool versus a message queue-based approach.
8. Ensure that the system is compatible with multiple platforms and operating systems, including Linux, Windows, macOS, and embedded systems like ARM and RISC-V architectures. Test the system on various platforms to ensure compatibility and performance.

To implement this system, we will need to use a combination of design patterns and software engineering principles, such as plugin architecture, dependency management, resource pooling, thread safety, and asynchronous logging. We will also need to evaluate the performance impact of different design choices and optimize the system for high-throughput logging scenarios.

Here is an example of how this might be implemented in C:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>

// Plugin architecture for logging backends
typedef struct {
  const char* name;
  void (*init)(void);
  int (*log)(const char* message);
} LogBackendPlugin;

LogBackendPlugin* plugin_list = NULL;
int num_plugins = 0;

// Resource pool for logging mechanisms
typedef struct {
  void* handle;
  const char* name;
  int (*log)(const char* message);
} LogResource;

LogResource* resource_pool = NULL;
int num_resources = 0;

// Thread-safe queue for logging messages
typedef struct {
  void* data;
  struct LogMessageQueue* next;
} LogMessageQueue;

LogMessageQueue* message_queue = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t queue_lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Initialize the logging manager
void init_logging() {
  // Load all logging plugins dynamically
  num_plugins = load_logging_plugins();
  plugin_list = malloc(sizeof(LogBackendPlugin) * num_plugins);

  // Allocate resources for each plugin
  for (int i = 0; i < num_plugins; i++) {
    LogBackendPlugin* plugin = &plugin_list[i];
    void* handle = dlopen(plugin->name, RTLD_LAZY);
    if (!handle) {
      printf("Failed to load plugin %s\n", plugin->name);
      continue;
    }
    plugin->init();
    LogResource* resource = malloc(sizeof(LogResource));
    resource->handle = handle;
    resource->name = plugin->name;
    resource->log = plugin->log;
    resource_pool = realloc(resource_pool, sizeof(LogResource) * (num_resources + 1));
    resource_pool[num_resources] = resource;
    num_resources++;
  }
}

// Log a message to all registered logging mechanisms
void log_message(const char* message) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&queue_lock);
  if (!message_queue) {
    message_queue = malloc(sizeof(LogMessageQueue));
    message_queue->data = strdup(message);
    message_queue->next = NULL;
  } else {
    LogMessageQueue* q = message_queue;
    while (q->next != NULL) {
      q = q->next;
    }
    q->next = malloc(sizeof(LogMessageQueue));
    q->next->data = strdup(message);
    q->next->next = NULL;
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&queue_lock);
}

// Process all queued log messages in a non-blocking manner
void process_messages() {
  while (true) {
    // Acquire the lock to check if there are any messages to process
    pthread_mutex_lock(&queue_lock);
    LogMessageQueue* q = message_queue;
    if (!q) {
      break;
    }

    // Process all queued messages
    while (q->next != NULL) {
      q = q->next;
      for (int i = 0; i < num_resources; i++) {
        LogResource* resource = &resource_pool[i];
        if (!resource || !resource->log) continue;
        char* message = (char*)q->data;
        int result = resource->log(message);
        if (result != 0) {
          printf("Logging failed for %s\n", resource->name);
        }
      }
    }

    // Release the lock and check if there are any more messages to process
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&queue_lock);
  }
}
```
This is just an example, and you will need to modify it to fit your specific requirements. Additionally, you may want to consider using a logging framework such as the one provided by Apache Commons Logging for Java or the Python logging library to simplify the implementation of logging functionality.']

