Here is an example of how you could implement the `processFinancialTransactions` function in Java:
```
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock;

public class ProcessFinancialTransactions {
  private static final int MAX_ACCOUNT_ID_LENGTH = 10;
  private static final double TRANSACTION_FEE_RATE = 0.05;
  private static final double OVERDRAFT_LIMIT = 1000;
  private static final long QUERY_BOUNDS = 1000L;

  private Map<String, Account> accounts;
  private Lock readWriteLock;

  public ProcessFinancialTransactions() {
    this.accounts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    this.readWriteLock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
  }

  public void initializeAccount(String accountId, double balance) {
    readWriteLock.writeLock().lock();
    try {
      accounts.put(accountId, new Account(balance));
    } finally {
      readWriteLock.writeLock().unlock();
    }
  }

  public void deposit(String accountId, double amount) throws InvalidTransactionException {
    readWriteLock.readLock().lock();
    try {
      Account account = accounts.get(accountId);
      if (account == null) {
        throw new InvalidTransactionException("Invalid account ID");
      }
      account.deposit(amount);
    } finally {
      readWriteLock.readLock().unlock();
    }
  }

  public void withdraw(String accountId, double amount) throws InsufficientFundsException, InvalidTransactionException {
    readWriteLock.writeLock().lock();
    try {
      Account account = accounts.get(accountId);
      if (account == null) {
        throw new InvalidTransactionException("Invalid account ID");
      }
      account.withdraw(amount);
    } finally {
      readWriteLock.writeLock().unlock();
    }
  }

  public double getBalance(String accountId) throws InvalidTransactionException {
    readWriteLock.readLock().lock();
    try {
      Account account = accounts.get(accountId);
      if (account == null) {
        throw new InvalidTransactionException("Invalid account ID");
      }
      return account.getBalance();
    } finally {
      readWriteLock.readLock().unlock();
    }
  }

  public void processTransactions(List<Transaction> transactions) throws InvalidTransactionException {
    for (Transaction transaction : transactions) {
      switch (transaction.getType()) {
        case DEPOSIT:
          deposit(transaction.getAccountId(), transaction.getAmount());
          break;
        case WITHDRAWAL:
          withdraw(transaction.getAccountId(), transaction.getAmount());
          break;
        default:
          throw new InvalidTransactionException("Invalid transaction type");
      }
    }
  }
}
```
This implementation of the `ProcessFinancialTransactions` class uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` to store the accounts and a `ReentrantReadWriteLock` to ensure thread safety. It also includes methods for initializing accounts, depositing and withdrawing funds, getting the balance of an account, and processing transactions.

To implement the fraud detection module, you could create a separate class that analyzes transaction patterns in real-time and identifies potential fraudulent activities. This class could use machine learning algorithms or other techniques to analyze the transaction data and determine whether there are any suspicious patterns or anomalies that could indicate fraudulent activity.

To incorporate the caching mechanism, you could create a separate `Cache` class that stores frequently accessed account details in memory. The cache could be periodically updated based on the current balance of each account to ensure that it remains up-to-date.

To support batch processing of transaction serialization and deserialization, you could use a library such as Jackson or GSON to serialize and deserialize the transactions in bulk. This would help reduce I/O latency and optimize the use of disk resources.

Finally, to ensure that the function can handle sudden increases in load and maintain performance under high stress conditions, you could consider using a load balancer or a distributed computing framework such as Apache Spark or Hadoop to process the transactions in parallel across multiple nodes. This would help distribute the workload evenly and improve the overall performance of the system.']

