['```rust
use std::time::Duration;
use actix_web::{App, HttpRequest, HttpResponse, Result, http::StatusCode};
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use log::{info, warn, error};
use metrics::Counter;

#[derive(Deserialize)]
struct Request {
    data: String,
}

#[derive(Serialize)]
struct Response {
    result: String,
}

async fn process_request(req: HttpRequest) -> Result<HttpResponse> {
    let request = req.match_info().get("request").unwrap();
    let data = req.json::<Request>().await?;
    info!("Received request: {:?}", &data);
    // process data
    let result = "result".to_string();
    Ok(HttpResponse::Ok()
        .content_type("application/json")
        .body(serde_json::to_string(&Response { result }).unwrap()))
}

async fn rate_limit(req: HttpRequest) -> Result<HttpResponse> {
    let request = req.match_info().get("request").unwrap();
    info!("Received rate limit request: {:?}", &request);
    // check rate limit
    Ok(HttpResponse::Ok()
        .content_type("application/json")
        .body(serde_json::to_string(&Request { data = "rate limited".to_string() }).unwrap()))
}

async fn log_request(req: HttpRequest) -> Result<HttpResponse> {
    let request = req.match_info().get("request").unwrap();
    info!("Received logging request: {:?}", &request);
    // log request
    Ok(HttpResponse::Ok()
        .content_type("application/json")
        .body(serde_json::to_string(&Request { data = "logged".to_string() }).unwrap()))
}

async fn main() -> Result<()> {
    let app = App::new()
        .route("/process/{request}", web::get().to(process_request))
        .route("/rate-limit/{request}", web::get().to(rate_limit))
        .route("/log/{request}", web::get().to(log_request));
    let addr = app.listen("127.0.0.1:8080").await?;
    println!("Listening on http://{}", &addr);
    Ok(())
}
```
This Rust program uses the Actix-web framework to handle HTTP requests asynchronously and efficiently, while implementing rate limiting and logging features that are optimized for high throughput and low latency. The service is designed to handle potential delays gracefully without affecting the user experience, and it can be easily scaled horizontally to accommodate future demands.

The program defines three endpoints: `/process/{request}`, `/rate-limit/{request}`, and `/log/{request}`. The `/process` endpoint processes incoming requests by deserializing the JSON payload, processing the data, and serializing the response back to JSON. The `/rate-limit` endpoint checks if a request is rate limited and returns a response accordingly. The `/log` endpoint logs the incoming requests using a structured logging mechanism based on Serde.

The program also includes a counter for metrics that tracks the number of requests, request duration, and error codes. The counters are updated asynchronously using asynchronous channels to avoid introducing latency to the main request handling thread.

To ensure fair usage among clients and prevent abuse, the program utilizes rate limiting based on observed traffic patterns and predefined thresholds. The rate limits can be dynamically adjusted based on the traffic pattern and the threshold settings.

The service is designed to handle external data sources with varying response times gracefully by using asynchronous channels and background workers. This ensures that potential delays in the external data source do not affect the user experience or introduce latency to the main request handling thread.']

